<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Bulgaria</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Bulgarian</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Bulgaria is a country in southeastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a> whose medieval Bulgarian states were major rivals of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantium</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> reconquered Bulgaria in the early eleventh century, restoring the Byzantine Empire's control of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, and Bulgaria later adopted Byzantine <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Christianity</a> and culture. Under Ottoman rule from the fourteenth century, it gained autonomy after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–1878, part of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a>'s decline.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bulgaria.jpg" alt="Bulgaria" width="320" height="192" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: SKopp · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Flag_of_Bulgaria.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Bulgaria covers 110,994 square kilometres in the east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a>, south of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/danube">Danube</a> and west of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-sea">Black Sea</a>, with a coastline of 354 kilometres. Its main topographical features are the Danubian Plain, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkan-mountains">Balkan Mountains</a>, the Upper Thracian Plain and the Rila-Rhodope massif. The Balkan Mountains run from west to east through the middle of the country, and Musala, at 2,925 metres, is the highest point in both Bulgaria and the Balkans. Plains occupy about a third of the territory and plateaux and hills 41%. The climate is varied and changeable because the country lies at the meeting point of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mediterranean">Mediterranean</a>, Oceanic and Continental air masses.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Neanderthal remains of around 150,000 years ago are among the earliest traces of human activity, and the Karanovo culture arose c. 6,500 BCE. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/varna">Varna</a> culture is credited with the invention of goldworking and produced the oldest golden jewellery in the world, over 6,000 years old. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a> appeared on the Balkan Peninsula before the 12th century BCE, and after the defeat of the second Persian invasion in 479 BCE most of the tribes united under king Teres to form the Odrysian kingdom.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/philip-ii-of-macedon">Philip II of Macedon</a> vassalised it in 341 BCE, and it became a Roman province in 45 CE. In the 6th century the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/slavs">Slavs</a> entered the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> and assimilated the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/thracians">Thracians</a>.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgars">Bulgars</a>, led by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asparuh-of-bulgaria">Asparuh</a>, permanently invaded the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/balkans">Balkans</a> in the late 7th century and established the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire</a>, recognised by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire</a> in 681. It dominated most of the Balkans and influenced Slavic cultures through the Cyrillic script, which the Preslav Literary School developed. It lasted until the early 11th century, when the Byzantine emperor <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basil-ii">Basil II</a> conquered and dismantled it.</p><p>A revolt in 1185 established the Second Bulgarian Empire, which reached its apex under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ivan-asen-ii">Ivan Asen II</a> (1218–1241), and in 1396 the empire, weakened by wars and feudal strife, fell under Ottoman rule for nearly five centuries.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russo-turkish-war-1877-1878">Russo-Turkish War</a> of 1877–78 resulted in the third and current Bulgarian state, which declared independence from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottoman Empire</a> in 1908. Many ethnic <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bulgarians">Bulgarians</a> were left outside its borders, which stoked irredentist sentiment, conflicts with neighbours and alliances with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a> in both world wars.</p><p>In 1946 Bulgaria came under the Soviet-led Eastern Bloc as a socialist state, and the Communist Party gave up its monopoly on power after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/revolutions-of-1989">revolutions of 1989</a>. It has been a parliamentary republic of 28 provinces since the constitution of 1991, and a member of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/european-union">European Union</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nato">NATO</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Bulgaria” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Basil II (r. 976–1025) used these resources to conquer <b>Bulgaria</b> after decades of war, earning the epithet Bulgar-slayer, and left the empire stretching from the Danube to northern Syria, its greatest extent since the seventh century.</blockquote><a href="/empire/byzantine-empire">Byzantine Empire · Military</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Bulgaria</b> also gave an example of a Christian Slavic monarchy modelled on Byzantium, with its own patriarch and tsar.</blockquote><a href="/empire/first-bulgarian-empire">First Bulgarian Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>A morganatic branch, the Battenbergs, produced Alexander, the first Prince of <b>Bulgaria</b>, and Louis of Battenberg, First Sea Lord of the Royal Navy, whose descendants took the name Mountbatten.</blockquote><a href="/empire/grand-duchy-of-hesse">Grand Duchy of Hesse · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>…the Slavic liturgy lasted only about 22 years in Moravia, the disciples expelled in 886 carried it to <b>Bulgaria</b>, Croatia and Bohemia; at the Preslav Literary School Bulgarian disciples such as Naum and Clement of Ohrid created the Cyrillic alphabet, which became the script of the Bulgarian Empire…</blockquote><a href="/empire/great-moravia">Great Moravia · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>The Georgian Orthodox Church was the largest landowner after the crown, and Georgian monasteries flourished abroad, notably the Iviron on Mount Athos, the Monastery of the Cross in Jerusalem and Petritsoni in <b>Bulgaria</b>, linking the kingdom to the wider Orthodox world.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-georgia">Kingdom of Georgia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>…at Goudi in 1909, the army and navy performed well in the Balkan Wars of 1912–13: Greek forces entered Thessaloniki on 8 November 1912, took Ioannina in 1913, and defeated <b>Bulgaria</b> in the Second Balkan War, while the fleet commanded by Pavlos Kountouriotis won control of the Aegean.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-greece">Kingdom of Greece · Military</a></li></ul></section>
